      "text": "Olive Oil Ice Cream, circulator\n    \n  \n  Scaled 0.8× from the 1 kg formula. All ratios unchanged — PAC, POD, fat, MSNF and total solids are identical to the full batch. Sized to two 16 oz Ball jars in a circulator bath.\n  Rename\n\n\n  VersionShow details\n  \n    Version 1 · 50 g oil · 800 g · Latest\n    Draft: Version 2 · less oil\n    \n  \n  Next version\n\n  \n  HistoryShow2 versions\n  \n\nOlive Oil Ice CreamSilky and quietly savoury. Fresh olive oil adds a gentle fruitiness without overwhelming the cream, and a little more salt than you would think carries it. Serve it soft, with flaky salt.IngredientsIngredientAs made% of batchStep 2Gum slurry — the only high-heat step120 gWhole milk estimated120 g of 370.4 g · 46.3% in all120 g15.0%12 gSucrose12 g of 76.0 g · 9.5% in all1.5%1.04 gLocust bean gum0.1%0.48 gGuar gum0.1%0.16 gLambda carrageenantraceStep 3Build the base250.4 gWhole milk estimated250.4 g of 370.4 g · 46.3% in all263 g31.3%252.8 gHeavy cream estimated241 g31.6%22.4 gSkim milk powder2.8%64 gSucrose64 g of 76.0 g · 9.5% in all8.0%12 gDextrose1.5%3.2 gFine sea salt estimated0.4%Step 6Allulose in, then crash-cool20 gAllulose estimated2.5%Step 8Emulsify the oil, cold40 gGraza Drizzle45 g5.0%1.2 gSoy lecithin0 g0.2%799.7 gTotal804.3 gAs made totals what was written; the plan fills in where nothing was.InstructionsBefore you startTaste the Graza straight. Polyphenols degrade with light and oxygen, and the squeeze bottle offers less protection than dark glass. An old bottle at 40 g will disappear entirely.Check the cream’s actual butterfat. The carton states a 36% minimum, which is a floor.1Lecithin into the oil. Whisk 1.2 g soy lecithin into the 40 g of Drizzle. Cover, leave at room temperature. SkippedtemproomIt's lipophilic — it disperses poorly if added to the water phase.2Gum slurry — the only high-heat step. Toss 1.68 g of the gum blend with the sucrose. Whisk into the milk in a small saucepan. Heat, whisking constantly, then pull off.temp85 °Chold2 minAt 120 g this is a thin layer in the pan. It scorches and evaporates faster than a larger volume would — keep whisking and don’t walk away.3Build the base. Whisk the remaining sucrose, plus 22.4 g SMP, 12 g dextrose and 3.2 g salt, into the remaining milk and all 252.8 g of cream. Add the hot gum slurry. Immersion blend.blend60 s4Divide. Tare, pour, check.per jar379 gMismatched jars hit core temperature at different times and you would be timing off the wrong one.Two-piece lids finger-tight only — the headspace air expands and needs to vent rather than build pressure.5Pasteurise in the circulator. Start the clock when the jar core reaches temperature.temp69 °Chold40 mincome-up10–12 minWith 379 g in a squat wide-mouth jar the come-up is much faster than a single large vessel.Probe the core rather than trusting the estimate. Invert each jar once or twice partway through.6Allulose in, then crash-cool. Combine both jars, stir in the 20 g allulose off the heat until dissolved, then straight into an ice bath.tobelow 5 °CAllulose skips the hold — it browns readily with milk proteins.7Age. Hold covered in the fridge.temp4 °Ctime12–24 h8Emulsify the oil, cold. Mix still at 4 °C. Pour the lecithin-oil blend in a thin stream under a running immersion blender.temp4 °Cblend45 sNever heat this oil.blend 60 s9Churn. Freeze immediately.overrun25–30%Fast, Soft, Prechill 15 min. Speed Δ @ 20 min, really thick @ 24, full churn 3010Harden, and serve warm. Harden, then temper before serving.harden−20 °C, 4+ hserve−11 to −12 °CBalanceShowWatch forShow\n\n  \n    Batchchurned 2 Aug 2026\n    CorrectRecord another\n  \n  \n    Time to draw temp.20 minplan 10–12 min\n    Out of machine−6 °C\n    Churn duration30 min\n    Exit consistencynot measured\n    Airinessnot measured\n  \n  Soft, not greasyoil bottle opened 24 Jul\n  \n    TastingShowtasted date unknown\n  \n  Recorded 4 Aug 2026 against Version 1 · 50 g oil · 800 g"
